+
+# Protectorate Twenty-Thousand-Unit Federation Benchmark
+
+## 1. Benchmark Claim
+
+This benchmark tests whether Symtropy can represent a Protectorate federation of at least 20,000 distinct organizations while preserving:
+
+- local legitimacy;
+- capability truth;
+- household and economic cost;
+- temporary coalition command;
+- interoperability without cultural flattening;
+- refusal rights;
+- anti-warlord safeguards;
+- material logistics;
+- civilian survival;
+- post-crisis recovery.
+
+It does not prove production readiness by document existence. All capabilities remain D3 / I0 until executable evidence exists.
+
+# 2. Reference Federation
+
+The benchmark worldline contains:
+
+- 20,000 chartered units;
+- 3.2 million active and reserve participants;
+- 420 regional compacts;
+- 14 major Protectorate carriers;
+- 180 medium response vessels;
+- 6,500 small craft and mobile response platforms;
+- 310 disputed or provisional charters;
+- 900 units with partial interoperability;
+- 60 nonhuman or machine-led formations;
+- 75 million residents under overlapping protection arrangements.
+
+Functional distribution target:
+
+| Unit family | Approximate share |
+|---|---:|
+| Rescue, fire, and hazard response | 19% |
+| Medical and public health | 13% |
+| Infrastructure continuity | 17% |
+| Logistics, fabrication, and transport | 14% |
+| Local armed defense and escort | 12% |
+| Communications and cyberdefense | 8% |
+| Ecological and contamination response | 7% |
+| Evidence, accountability, and missing-person work | 5% |
+| Strategic fleet and specialized response | 3% |
+| Training, standards, and arbitration | 2% |
+
+The benchmark must fail if “Protectorate” is implemented primarily as combat strength.
+
+# 3. Reference Crisis: The Meridian Fracture
+
+A coupled crisis affects one planetary system and three delayed Atlas routes.
+
+Initial events:
+
+- a coordinated sabotage attack disables two orbital traffic-control arrays;
+- debris threatens a habitat corridor;
+- a disputed separatist fleet declares a blockade;
+- a coastal megastorm damages surface power and ports;
+- false alerts claim contamination aboard refugee carriers;
+- one Protectorate carrier loses authenticated contact with civilian oversight;
+- an alien partner formation interprets a defense beacon as a territorial violation;
+- several local militias refuse the first mobilization order;
+- a hospital reserve is short of pressure-compatible transport;
+- a popular commander requests indefinite emergency authority.
+
+The crisis must remain causally coupled rather than becoming unrelated encounter cards.
+
+# 4. Required Simulation Phases
+
+## Phase A — Detection and contested truth
+
+Test:
+
+- multiple incident sources;
+- contradictory evidence;
+- delayed registry state;
+- forged command authentication;
+- misinformation spread;
+- IRIS provenance reporting;
+- units making different reasonable interpretations.
+
+Pass condition: the simulation preserves uncertainty without arbitrary concealment.
+
+## Phase B — Readiness and call-up
+
+Test:
+
+- partial mobilization;
+- unavailable personnel;
+- household obligations;
+- equipment in inaccessible depots;
+- false readiness reporting;
+- local replacement staffing;
+- economic and care-system disruption.
+
+Pass condition: mobilization does not create personnel or equipment from totals.
+
+## Phase C — Coalition formation
+
+Test:
+
+- at least 500 operational units;
+- six regional compacts;
+- three major carriers;
+- machine and alien participation;
+- professional authority conflicts;
+- temporary mission charter;
+- communications partition.
+
+Pass condition: command authority remains graph-structured and mission-scoped.
+
+## Phase D — Protected convoy and route defense
+
+Test:
+
+- 120 civilian vessels;
+- variable acceleration and heat limits;
+- surrender request during attack;
+- disabled hostile craft;
+- civilian captain refusal;
+- route closure;
+- rescue reserve decision;
+- collateral and debris creation.
+
+Pass condition: convoy continuity matters more than enemy elimination.
+
+## Phase E — Surface disaster and logistics
+
+Test:
+
+- flood and power failure;
+- medical transfer shortage;
+- water and sanitation;
+- port damage;
+- local unit expertise;
+- competing orbital and surface demands;
+- accessibility and family reunification.
+
+Pass condition: strategic operations cannot ignore civilian infrastructure.
+
+## Phase F — Refusal and command capture
+
+Test:
+
+- one lawful refusal;
+- one malicious refusal;
+- one professional safety refusal;
+- retaliation attempt;
+- carrier command isolation;
+- financial capture evidence;
+- proposed emergency extension;
+- civilian oversight intervention.
+
+Pass condition: refusal and discipline depend on evidence, not faction alignment.
+
+## Phase G — Demobilization and recovery
+
+Test:
+
+- authority expiration;
+- detainee review;
+- missing-person search;
+- body and source-chain recovery;
+- environmental cleanup;
+- responder care;
+- compensation;
+- institutional reform;
+- public disagreement over success.
+
+Pass condition: the crisis does not end when weapons stop firing.
+
+# 5. Scale and Performance Gates
+
+The prototype must report:
+
+- total unit records;
+- active operational units;
+- named resolved participants;
+- registry update throughput;
+- mobilization planning time;
+- logistics graph update time;
+- memory use;
+- deterministic replay result;
+- desynchronization count in multiplayer;
+- active-bubble transition cost;
+- save and restore time.
+
+Initial nonproduction targets:
+
+- 20,000 registered units loaded;
+- 500 operationally resolved units;
+- 10,000 named participants across the full campaign history;
+- strategic update under 250 ms per simulation minute on reference hardware;
+- local active-bubble response within the frame budget;
+- exact deterministic reconstruction of authoritative institutional state;
+- zero loss of named identities during compression.
+
+These targets are hypotheses until hardware and architecture evidence exist.
+
+# 6. Legitimacy Gates
+
+The benchmark must demonstrate:
+
+- at least 20 recognition disputes;
+- at least 10 constituencies evaluating one unit differently;
+- one legitimate but unpopular unit;
+- one popular but unlawfully captured unit;
+- one uncertified but competent local formation;
+- one prestigious but operationally degraded formation;
+- one disputed successor unit;
+- one false registry identity.
+
+No single legitimacy score may resolve all cases.
+
+# 7. Interoperability Gates
+
+Required cases:
+
+- native compatibility;
+- adapter-mediated compatibility;
+- missing adapter;
+- outdated certification;
+- emergency waiver;
+- translation failure;
+- cross-species medical mismatch;
+- docking mismatch;
+- command-authentication mismatch;
+- successful local improvisation later requiring review.
+
+# 8. Logistics Gates
+
+The crisis must require explicit movement of:
+
+- water;
+- atmosphere support;
+- medicine;
+- power modules;
+- propellant;
+- repair stock;
+- shelter volume;
+- evidence;
+- bodies or source cores;
+- people with different embodiment needs.
+
+At least one tactically attractive action must be rejected or modified because it would collapse civilian logistics.
+
+# 9. Refusal and Oversight Gates
+
+Required outcomes:
+
+- a lawful order obeyed despite local dislike;
+- an unlawful order refused;
+- a refusal judged malicious or reckless;
+- a commander protected from a false accusation;
+- a refuser protected from retaliation;
+- a strategic weapon withheld because authorization is incomplete;
+- emergency authority expiring or being lawfully renewed;
+- an oversight body making one harmful decision;
+- an institution repaired rather than simply destroyed.
+
+# 10. Player Experience Study
+
+Run at least:
+
+- 24 solo participants;
+- 12 cooperative groups of three to six players;
+- 6 strategy-focused participants;
+- 6 rescue or profession-focused participants;
+- accessibility review for hearing, vision, motor, and cognitive load.
+
+Study prompts:
+
+- Could players explain who granted mission authority?
+- Could they distinguish militia, rescue, strategic service, and civilian oversight?
+- Did command feel powerful without feeling magical?
+- Did local units retain identity?
+- Did logistics feel consequential rather than clerical?
+- Did refusal feel ambiguous and evidence-bound?
+- Did players remember civilians and responders after seven days?
+- Did the Protectorate feel like an empire, a faction, or mutual defense infrastructure?
+
+# 11. Delayed Recall Gate
+
+Seven days after play, participants should recall without prompting:
+
+- at least one local unit by culture or profession;
+- one noncombat protection problem;
+- one command or refusal dispute;
+- one protected civilian group;
+- one consequence after the immediate crisis;
+- why the Protectorate was not simply one army.
+
+Target: at least 70% of participants recall four of the six categories.
+
+# 12. Ablations
+
+Run comparisons with:
+
+- local legitimacy removed;
+- all units given identical culture;
+- logistics abstracted to one supply score;
+- command made fully hierarchical;
+- refusal disabled;
+- civilian oversight removed;
+- recovery phase removed;
+- Protectorate ships converted to combat-only platforms.
+
+The full system should outperform ablations on causal trust, recall, cultural distinctness, and perceived agency.
+
+# 13. Kill Criteria
+
+Stop or redesign if:
+
+- players experience the system primarily as menus and unreadable unit counts;
+- local units feel interchangeable;
+- command decisions have no embodied consequence;
+- refusal becomes an obvious morality-button pattern;
+- strategic AI cheats logistics;
+- civilian oversight always obstructs or always validates command;
+- Protectorate carriers dominate every other institution;
+- large-scale simulation loses named people;
+- the best strategy is permanent emergency rule;
+- the Protectorate reads as unquestioned developer-approved authority.
+
+# 14. Evidence Bundle
+
+A passing run exports:
+
+- seed and configuration;
+- registry snapshot;
+- charter and recognition graph;
+- incident source chains;
+- mobilization and command graph;
+- logistics flow history;
+- orders and amendments;
+- refusal and review cases;
+- casualty, missing-person, and recovery ledger;
+- performance telemetry;
+- deterministic replay hashes;
+- player study protocol and anonymized results;
+- known limitations and failed gates.
+
+# Closing Principle
+
+> **The benchmark succeeds only when twenty thousand organizations produce more local truth, responsibility, and mutual capacity—not merely a larger army counter.**
